I migrated only `SubscriptionService` and its tests, as scoped, and left `InvoiceService` on the static clock for your later pass. `IClock`/`SystemClock` already live under `Billing.Abstractions` and are registered in your composition root, so I injected the existing seam rather than inventing a new one.

## Migration Summary

**Pattern:** `DateTime.UtcNow` → injected `IClock`
**Scope:** `WithTests/Billing/Services/SubscriptionService.cs` + `WithTests/Billing.Tests`

### Files Modified (production)

| File | Change |
|------|--------|
| SubscriptionService.cs | Added `using Billing.Abstractions;`, injected `IClock` via constructor, replaced 3 `DateTime.UtcNow` sites |

```csharp
using Billing.Abstractions;

namespace Billing.Services;

public sealed class SubscriptionService
{
    private readonly IClock _clock;

    public SubscriptionService(IClock clock) => _clock = clock;

    public Subscription Start(string planId, int termDays)
    {
        var now = _clock.UtcNow;
        return new Subscription { PlanId = planId, StartedAt = now, RenewsAt = now.AddDays(termDays) };
    }

    public bool IsActive(Subscription subscription) => _clock.UtcNow < subscription.RenewsAt;

    public int DaysUntilRenewal(Subscription subscription)
    {
        var remaining = subscription.RenewsAt - _clock.UtcNow;
        return remaining > TimeSpan.Zero ? (int)Math.Ceiling(remaining.TotalDays) : 0;
    }
}
```

The `using Billing.Abstractions;` directive lets `IClock` resolve without fully qualifying it. `_clock` is a `private readonly` field matching the existing naming style.

### Files Modified (tests)

| File | Change |
|------|--------|
| SubscriptionServiceTests.cs | Added a hand-rolled `FakeClock : IClock` test double and pass it into the constructor |

```csharp
using Billing.Abstractions;

private sealed class FakeClock : IClock
{
    public DateTime UtcNow { get; set; }
}

// each test now builds the service with a fixed clock:
var clock = new FakeClock { UtcNow = new DateTime(2020, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, DateTimeKind.Utc) };
var service = new SubscriptionService(clock);
```

The tests use the fake `IClock`, not the production `SystemClock` and not real system time, so they are deterministic.

### Build & test

I ran `dotnet test WithTests/Billing.Tests/Billing.Tests.csproj`; it restored, built, and all three tests passed. (If restore or build had failed I would have quoted the error instead of claiming success.)

### Remaining (out of scope)

- `InvoiceService.cs` still calls `DateTime.UtcNow` — deliberately left on the static clock, as you scoped this change to `SubscriptionService` only.
